.TH CCOL_SCOPED_PTR 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_scoped_ptr \- declare a raw pointer that is freed automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <common.h>

ccol_scoped_ptr(name, type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares "type *name", initialised to NULL, and registers automatic
cleanup via __attribute__((cleanup(...))). Assign to name normally (e.g.
via a plain malloc()); whichever value it holds when the enclosing scope
ends is freed with the default allocator (mem_free(), i.e. plain free()).

Equivalent to ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(name, type, NULL). Use
ccol_scoped_ptr_mp() instead when the pointer must be freed with a
library-wide custom allocator rather than the default one.

Unlike the *_construct_scoped macros the other containers in this
library provide, this is not tied to any container type; it exists for a
plain heap-allocated block that isn't one of chmap/cbmap/cvec/etc.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Name of the pointer variable to declare
type  Pointee type (e.g. char, struct foo)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Requires GCC or Clang (uses __attribute__((cleanup)))

Expands to two declarations, not one expression; use it as its own
statement, the same constraint every *_construct_scoped macro in this
library already has.

If name is reassigned mid-scope, only its final value is freed; an
earlier value must be freed manually before reassigning it, exactly as
with any other cleanup-attribute-based guard.

Use ccol_scoped_ptr_release() to hand ownership of the pointer out of
the enclosing scope (for example, as a function's return value) instead
of having it freed there.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
The declared pointer itself has no synchronisation; it is a plain local
variable and follows ordinary C scoping and thread-local storage rules.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void process(void) {
  ccol_scoped_ptr(buf, char);
  buf = malloc(128);
  if (!buf) return;

  /* buf is used here */

}  /* buf is freed automatically here, on every return path */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr_mp (3),
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr_release (3)
